  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nTroughing over the Pacific NW will gradually strengthen over the\nnext couple days, increasing southwest flow over the region. This\nalong with persistent easterly to southeasterly winds at the low\nlevels will work to increase atmospheric moisture values and\nincrease chances for showers and thunderstorms. Ensembles depict \nweak energy moving up under southwest flow this afternoon and \nevening. The most favorable area for strong to severe \nthunderstorms will be southeast MT where short term ensembles \ndepict 1000-1500 J/kg of MUCAPE and 30-40 kts of bulk shear. The \nmain threats with any severe thunderstorms will be heavy rain, \nlarge hail and strong winds. With precipitable water values \n150-200% of normal and a few slow moving storms expected, the \nthreat for localized heavy rain will be a concern Wednesday and\nThursday. Into Thursday, instability and shear look to have \nslightly lesser values, but a few strong thunderstorms are \npossible across the area. With the main threat being heavy rain, \nthose recreating in or near burn scars should stay aware of \nchanging conditions regarding flash flooding. Scattered showers \nand isolated embedded thunderstorms look to keep low to moderate \nchances for precip Friday and Saturday across the plains with high\nchances across area mountains. The NBM gives about a 60-90% \nchance of a half an inch of precipitation Thursday through Friday \nwith about a 60-70% chance for an inch for area mountains and \nsoutheast MT. Ensembles then show weak ridging starting to build \nback in beginning next week. \n\nTemperatures will gradually cool over the next couple days with\nhighs forecast to be in the 80s today then 70s to low 80s \nThursday through Saturday. Temps will increase back into the 80s \nfor next week. TS",
